    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a really hard time with my signals class. i get the formulas for the fourier transform but i dont really get the actual intuition. like how can one single wave just be made up of a bunch of other waves? can u explain it in a way that actually makes sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of any complex wave like a chord on a guitar; while it sounds like one single note, it is actually a combination of many pure frequency waves (harmonics) vibrating at once. The Fourier Transform acts like a radio tuner, allowing you to take that messy chord and separate out each individual frequency component hiding inside it. This works because mathematical functions called sinusoids have a unique ability to add up constructively or destructively, creating new shapes that look nothing like the individual waves that make them. Essentially, you are just breaking down a complex picture into its simpler, atomic color pixels to better understand what you're looking at.",
      "source": "target",
